window.WRAP911_DATA={modules:[{id:"safety",title:"Safety",icon:"🫡",summary:"Ladders, heat tools, knives, and shop awareness before any film goes down.",lesson:["WRAP 911 starts every job with safety—not film. Tall RV sides, box vans, and architectural walls put you on ladders and around open flame. Set ladders on level ground, lock spreaders, and keep three points of contact. Never stand on the top cap of an A-frame.","Stage or hinge large panels from the ground when you can. Do not climb while wrestling a full sheet of vinyl. Clear liner scraps and tape from under ladder feet so nobody slides mid-pull.","Heat tools (propane torch or heat gun) need a stable base when idle. Keep combustible scraps away from open flame. Knives stay sharp and controlled—cut into voids and gaps, never toward your body or across finished paint without a plan.","Forklifts, open bay doors, and other crews mean situational awareness. Know what is moving behind you. Platform first, then technique. Brands we use—3M, Avery Dennison, and BriteLine—do not replace jobsite common sense."],quiz:[{q:"What is the safest way to handle a full-height graphic on a tall RV side?",choices:["Stage or hinge the panel, keep the ladder stable and clear of debris, and avoid climbing while wrestling loose film.","Stand on the top cap of the A-frame so you can stretch the seam in one pass.","Climb with the full panel in both hands to save a trip."],answer:0},{q:"Where should a propane torch sit when you are not actively heating film?",choices:["On its side on the floor so the flame points away from the vehicle.","In a stable base on a clear bench or stand, away from liner scraps.","Hanging from the ladder rung by the hose."],answer:1}]}]};
